analysisWe need to produce an assert statement for the comment:

```
// @@@ If the index is within the valid range (greater or equal to 0 and less than the initial size of the vector), then for every index i less than the given index, the element at i remains unchanged and is either null or equal to its previous value.
```

We need to refer to the method remove(int index). The method modifies the vector: removes element at index, shifts later elements left, decrements elementCount, sets last slot to null.

The spec: If index is within valid range (>=0 && index < old size), then for every i < index, the element at i after removal remains unchanged and is either null or equal to its previous value.

We need to express: (index >=0 && index < \old(this.size())) => (\forall int i; 0 <= i && i < index; (this.elementAt(i) == null) || this.elementAt(i).equals(\old(this.elementAt(i))) ). However we must use only public observer methods. elementAt is public synchronized E elementAt(int index). Also size() is public. So we can use this.elementAt(i) and \old(this.elementAt(i)). Also note that elementAt may throw exception if out of range; but we are within range.

Spec also says "either null or equal to its previous value". So condition: this.elementAt(i) == null || this.elementAt(i).equals(\old(this.elementAt(i))). But need to handle null safely: maybe use (this.elementAt(i) == null ? true : this.elementAt(i).equals(\old(this.elementAt(i)))) . But typical spec uses (this.elementAt(i) == null) || this.elementAt(i).equals(\old(this.elementAt(i))). That's fine.

Thus final assert:

assert (index >= 0 && index < \old(this.size())) => (\forall int i; 0 <= i && i < index; (this.elementAt(i) == null) || this.elementAt(i).equals(\old(this.elementAt(i))));

Make sure parentheses correct: Use => lower precedence than &&, but we need parentheses around antecedent maybe not needed. Provide final code block.

