analysisWe need to produce an assert statement matching the comment. Let's parse comment:

```
// @@@ If the index is within the valid range (greater or equal to 0 and less than the initial size of the vector), then for every index i less than the given index, the element at i remains unchanged and is either null or equal to its previous value.
```

We need to refer to initial size before removal: \old(this.size()) maybe. The method is remove(int index). Preconditions: index >=0 && index < initial size. Then for all i with 0 <= i < index, the element at i after removal remains unchanged and is either null or equal to its previous value. Actually "remains unchanged and is either null or equal to its previous value." That seems contradictory: unchanged means same value; but they also say either null or equal to its previous value. Perhaps they want to assert that after removal, element at i is either null or equal to old element at i. But unchanged means equal to old, but maybe they consider that some positions could be null originally. So we assert: (index>=0 && index < \old(this.size())) => (\forall int i; 0 <= i && i < index; (this.elementAt(i) == null) || this.elementAt(i).equals(\old(this.elementAt(i))) ). Need to use public observer methods: elementAt(int) returns E, may be null. Use .equals? For objects we can use .equals but need to handle null. In earlier examples they used this.query(i).equals(\old(this.query(i))) . So they used .equals method. For null, .equals would throw NPE. In earlier they didn't handle null; but they said "All the valid indices ... have the same element after add". They used .equals directly. So we might follow same style: this.elementAt(i).equals(\old(this.elementAt(i))) . But need also allow null. Perhaps we can use (this.elementAt(i) == null ? \old(this.elementAt(i)) == null : this.elementAt(i).equals(\old(this.elementAt(i)))) . That's more precise.

Simplify: assert (index>=0 && index<\old(this.size())) => \forall int i; 0<=i && i<index; (this.elementAt(i)==null ? \old(this.elementAt(i))==null : this.elementAt(i).equals(\old(this.elementAt(i))));

But we need to use only public observer methods. elementAt is public synchronized E elementAt(int index). So okay.

Thus final answer:

